        </m:mrow>
      </m:annotation-xml>
    </m:semantics>
  </m:math>
</para>
<para>In other trinomials, don’t forget to look for a GCF first, and remember if the leading coefficient is negative,
  so is the GCF.</para>
<title>Try It: Factoring Trinomials Using Trial and Error</title>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Factor each trinomial completely using trial and error.</para>
    <para> \(5x^3 + 15x^2 − 20x\) </para>
    <para> \(10y^2 − 37y + 7\) </para>
    <para> \(6n^3 − 34n^2 + 40n\) </para>
    <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to factor trinomials using trial and error.</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> \(5x(x − 1)(x + 4)\) </item>
  </list>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> \((2y − 7)(5y − 1)\) </item>
  </list>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(2n(n − 4)(3n − 5)\) </item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
